const PASSAGE=`India's digital economy is changing the way citizens access financial services, education, healthcare and public information. Digital platforms can reduce transaction costs and make services available beyond traditional physical locations. However, access to technology alone does not guarantee meaningful inclusion. Users may face unreliable connectivity, low digital literacy, cyber risks or difficulty understanding online information. A sustainable digital ecosystem therefore needs both innovation and safeguards. Institutions must protect personal data, provide effective grievance mechanisms and design services that ordinary users can understand. At the same time, citizens need awareness about safe digital behaviour and responsible use of technology. The strongest systems are those that combine convenience with trust, because people are unlikely to adopt digital services confidently if they fear fraud or misuse of their information. Digital transformation should consequently be measured not only by the number of users or transactions, but also by whether technology makes services more accessible, secure and useful for different sections of society.`;
const QUESTIONS=["What benefits can digital platforms provide?","Why does technology access alone not guarantee inclusion?","Which risks can users face in a digital ecosystem?","What responsibilities do institutions have?","How should digital transformation ultimately be measured?"];
